# Build context exclusions for api/Dockerfile.
#
# The Dockerfile copies the csproj files, restores, then `COPY . .`. Without this
# file that second copy pulls in whatever the HOST has built: `bin/` and `obj/`
# from a local `dotnet build`, plus the diagnostic logs committed at the root of
# this folder. CI checks out fresh, so it never saw any of it — the cost lands
# only on a developer machine, which is the one place nobody is watching a build
# log.
#
# `obj/` is the one that actually breaks things rather than just bloating the
# context: it holds `project.assets.json` with absolute HOST paths and the host's
# SDK version, and a container restore that finds one can reuse it instead of
# resolving for the image.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow. Test projects are NOT excluded: every project in
# the .sln must be present for `dotnet restore` to succeed, and the publish step
# builds only the Api project and its dependencies, so they cost context and
# nothing else. Appsettings files are NOT excluded either — the image is expected
# to carry its defaults and take overrides from the environment.

# Host build output
**/bin/
**/obj/

# Diagnostic logs committed to api/ (tracked; see the roadmap item about them)
*.log

# Test output
**/TestResults/

# Editor and tooling state
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.user

# Local secrets, if one is ever placed here. The deployed stack takes its
# configuration from the environment.
.env
.env.*
